  <p class="herocap">Left: Xing and <b>Meng*</b>, <i>Ecological Indicators</i> (2020). Right: <b>Meng</b> et al., <i>Computers, Environment and Urban Systems</i> (2020).</p>

  <section id="question">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research question</p>
    <h2 class="sec">What do form and activity reveal about function and disadvantage?</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Official land use records state what a parcel is designated for, not what it does. They are revised infrequently, defined at administrative rather than behavioral boundaries, and silent on two properties that matter for population health: how intensely a place is used, and how disadvantaged the people who live there are. An exposure model built on designation alone therefore misses most of what distinguishes one neighborhood from another.</p>
      <p>The six studies collected here derive those properties from data the city already produces. Building footprints and road networks describe landscape form. Points of interest and place-based reviews describe activity and its timing. Street view imagery describes what a resident sees at eye level. Taken together they establish a sequence: urban function can be classified from physical form, refined by human activity, and then extended to measure vibrancy and material deprivation at parcel scale.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="approach">
    <p class="eyebrow">Approach</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Three moves across six studies</h2>
    <div class="cols3">
      <div class="step"><h3>Delineate</h3><p>Road networks and building-level blocks partition the city into functional regions and parcels. These are the units at which every subsequent measure is computed, so they are chosen to follow the street pattern rather than administrative lines.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Characterize</h3><p>Spatial metrics quantify the composition, shape, and aggregation of buildings, green land, and water within each unit. Topic models read the semantics and timing of points of interest and reviews; a segmentation network reads street view imagery.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Relate</h3><p>Random forests classify function against reference land use. Structural equation and geographically weighted regression models relate landscape composition to commercial pattern, to vibrancy, and to deprivation.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="form">
    <p class="eyebrow">Theme 1</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Reading function from urban form</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom:6px"><p>Both studies below ask how far physical form alone determines the function a parcel serves, and what has to be added when it does not.</p></div>

    <div class="study" id="s-ecolind">
      <p class="venue">Ecological Indicators &middot; 2020 &middot; corresponding author</p>
      <h3>Spatial metrics as a description of urban form</h3>
      <p class="cite">Xing, H., &amp; <b>Meng, Y.*</b> (2020). Measuring urban landscapes for urban function classification using spatial metrics. <i>Ecological Indicators</i>, 108, 105722.</p>
      <div class="prose"><p>Urban landscapes were represented as configurations of four element types, buildings, green land, water bodies, and other areas, within parcels delineated from the road network. Spatial metrics describing the composition, shape, and aggregation of those elements were computed for each parcel and used to classify residential, commercial and business, and public and service functions. A conditional inference random forest reached 0.813 classification accuracy and 0.757 testing accuracy, ahead of naive Bayes, support vector machines, and a standard random forest. Public and service functions remained the hardest class, which is the expected failure mode when a classifier sees only physical form.</p></div>
      <div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.110fr 2.133fr">
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ecolind_landscape-elements.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ecolind_landscape-elements.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="A single parcel showing its landscape elements: buildings, green land, water bodies, other areas, secondary roads, and points of interest" width="1351" height="1217" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>The landscape elements composing one parcel, the unit the metrics describe.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ecolind_function-classification.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ecolind_function-classification.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Two maps side by side showing classified urban functions and the reference land use data" width="2060" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Classified urban functions (left) against reference land use data (right).</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2019.105722">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/xingMeasuringUrbanLandscapes2020.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/xingMeasuringUrbanLandscapes2020.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="study" id="s-ceus18">
      <p class="venue">Computers, Environment and Urban Systems &middot; 2018 &middot; corresponding author</p>
      <h3>Adding socioeconomic evidence to physical form</h3>
      <p class="cite">Xing, H., &amp; <b>Meng, Y.*</b> (2018). Integrating landscape metrics and socioeconomic features for urban functional region classification. <i>Computers, Environment and Urban Systems</i>, 72, 134–145.</p>
      <div class="prose"><p>Eighteen landscape metrics were delineated from building-level blocks and combined with four socioeconomic features derived from 139,921 points of interest, then used by a random forest to classify functional regions delineated from the road network. Landscape metrics alone and socioeconomic features alone each reached 0.664 overall accuracy; used together they reached 0.818 with a kappa of 0.734. Physical form and social content are therefore complementary rather than substitutable, and a classifier given only one of them is leaving accuracy on the table.</p></div>
      <div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:0.620fr 1.090fr">
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2018_landscape-metrics.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2018_landscape-metrics.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Flow diagram showing building-level blocks and crowdsourced data feeding landscape metric delineation and socioeconomic feature extraction into a random forest classifier" width="1386" height="2234" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Landscape metrics and socioeconomic features entering a common classifier.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2018_function-classification.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2018_function-classification.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Four maps of functional regions classified as commercial, industrial, residential, and service or public land: ground truth, landscape metrics only, socioeconomic features only, and both combined" width="2400" height="2202" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Functional regions as ground truth (a), then classified with landscape metrics only (b), socioeconomic features only (c), and both together (d).</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2018.06.005">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/xingIntegratingLandscapeMetrics2018.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/xingIntegratingLandscapeMetrics2018.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="activity">
    <p class="eyebrow">Theme 2</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Reading function from human activity</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom:6px"><p>Physical form is static. The two studies below add what people do in a place, drawn from the timing and the semantics of what they post about it.</p></div>

    <div class="study" id="s-tgis">
      <p class="venue">Transactions in GIS &middot; 2018 &middot; corresponding author</p>
      <h3>Mixed land use from the rhythm of human activity</h3>
      <p class="cite">Xing, H., <b>Meng, Y.*</b>, &amp; Shi, Y. (2018). A dynamic human activity-driven model for mixed land use evaluation using social media data. <i>Transactions in GIS</i>, 22(5), 1130–1151.</p>
      <div class="prose"><p>Latent Dirichlet allocation extracted thirty topics from geotagged social media, each carrying a temporal profile as well as a semantic one. Topics with similar temporal distributions were clustered hierarchically, the clusters were then modified by semantic distance, and the result is a set of dynamic features describing when a parcel is used and for what. Shannon entropy over those features measured mixed land use. Validated against labelled buildings, dynamic features gave a Pearson correlation of 0.750, against 0.119 for semantic features alone: timing, not vocabulary, is what distinguishes one land use mixture from another.</p></div>
      <div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.353fr 1.417fr">
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/tgis_dynamic-model-workflow.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/tgis_dynamic-model-workflow.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Workflow diagram: social media data yields temporal and semantic information, topic modeling, temporal and semantic similarity clustering, and entropy calculation for mixed land use evaluation" width="1867" height="1380" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>The dynamic model: from social media posts to a mixed land use value per parcel.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/tgis_land-use-tagged-buildings.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/tgis_land-use-tagged-buildings.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Map of buildings colored by land use class across the study area" width="2231" height="1575" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Buildings tagged with land use classes, the reference against which the dynamic features were validated.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/tgis.12447">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/xingDynamicHumanActivitydriven2018.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/xingDynamicHumanActivitydriven2018.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="study" id="s-comm">
      <p class="venue">Computers, Environment and Urban Systems &middot; 2020 &middot; corresponding author</p>
      <h3>Commercial pattern as a function of the surrounding landscape</h3>
      <p class="cite">Xing, H., &amp; <b>Meng, Y.*</b> (2020). Revealing deep semantic commercial patterns: insights from urban landscape depiction. <i>Computers, Environment and Urban Systems</i>, 79, 101404.</p>
      <div class="prose"><p>Catering-related and accommodation-related commercial patterns were extracted as latent topics from place-based reviews, then related to the surrounding urban landscape through a structural equation model in which the landscape was represented by density of points of interest, percentage of building landscape, landscape shape index of buildings, and road density. The landscape carried standardized path weights of 0.705 on catering-related patterns and 0.236 on accommodation-related patterns. Commercial development is shaped by the physical environment around it, not only by employment and economic output, which is the premise a land use plan has to act on.</p></div>
      <div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:2.346fr 1.436fr">
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2020_sem-model.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2020_sem-model.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Structural equation model diagram linking density of points of interest, building landscape percentage, landscape shape index, and road density to catering-related and accommodation-related commercial patterns" width="2400" height="1023" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>The structural equation model relating urban landscape to commercial pattern.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2020_commercial-topics.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/ceus2020_commercial-topics.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Grid map with cells shaded blue for catering-related topics and red for accommodation-related topics" width="1370" height="954" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Grids where catering-related (blue) and accommodation-related (red) topics exceed a probability of 0.5.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2019.101404">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/xingRevealingDeepSemantic2020.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/xingRevealingDeepSemantic2020.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="outcome">
    <p class="eyebrow">Theme 3</p>
    <h2 class="sec">From function to social outcome</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom:6px"><p>The measures above describe places. The two studies below use them to describe people: how intensely a place is used, and how deprived the residents of it are. This is where the project meets population health.</p></div>

    <div class="study lead" id="s-cities">
      <span class="tag">Flagship</span>
      <p class="venue"><span class="j">Cities</span> &middot; 2019 &middot; first author</p>
      <h3>Landscape morphology and where a city is alive</h3>
      <p class="cite"><b>Meng, Y.</b>, &amp; Xing, H. (2019). Exploring the relationship between landscape characteristics and urban vibrancy: a case study using morphology and review data. <i>Cities</i>, 95, 102389.</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Urban vibrancy is usually approached through mixed land use, density, and accessibility. This study asked instead what the morphology of a place contributes, and described landscape characteristics at four levels: places, land use, single landscape elements, and multiple landscape elements. Vibrancy was measured by the density of place-based reviews, a behavioral trace of where people actually go rather than a demographic proxy for where they live.</p>
        <p>Regression models reached adjusted R-squared values of 0.65, 0.65, 0.66, and 0.67 across the four levels. Vibrancy is therefore substantially explained by morphology alone, and the gain from moving to richer descriptions of the landscape is real but modest, which tells a planner that coarse morphological description is already informative.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig-full" style="margin:18px 0 20px">
        <div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/cities2019_landscape-metrics.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/cities2019_landscape-metrics.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Twelve small maps, each showing the spatial distribution of one landscape metric across the parcels of the study area" width="2400" height="1536" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
        <figcaption>Twelve landscape metrics computed for every parcel, describing density, edge, patch structure, and building height.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.125fr 1.055fr">
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/cities2019_review-kernel-density.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/cities2019_review-kernel-density.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Kernel density map of place-based reviews with a 300 metre search radius" width="1952" height="1735" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Place-based reviews as a kernel density surface, 300 m search radius.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/cities2019_review-density-parcels.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/cities2019_review-density-parcels.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Map of review density aggregated within urban landscape parcels" width="1831" height="1735" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>The same reviews aggregated to landscape parcels, the unit used in the regression.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cities.2019.102389">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/mengExploringRelationshipLandscape2019.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/mengExploringRelationshipLandscape2019.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="study lead" id="s-poverty">
      <span class="tag">Flagship</span>
      <p class="venue"><span class="j">Computers, Environment and Urban Systems</span> &middot; 2020 &middot; first author</p>
      <h3>Street-level perception as a measure of deprivation</h3>
      <p class="cite"><b>Meng, Y.</b>, Xing, H., Yuan, Y., Wong, M. S., &amp; Fan, K. (2020). Sensing urban poverty: from the perspective of human perception-based greenery and open-space landscapes. <i>Computers, Environment and Urban Systems</i>, 84, 101544.</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Greenery measured from above is not the greenery a resident experiences. A PSPNet semantic segmentation network was applied to street view images captured at four headings per location and labelled sky, greenery, building, car, and road. From the segmented images, percentage indicators quantified how much greenery and open space a resident perceives, and aggregation indicators quantified how unevenly that perception is distributed within an area. Occupation and inequality are treated as separate properties, because two areas with the same average greenery can differ sharply in who has access to it.</p>
        <p>Geographically weighted regression related both indicators to the Index of Multiple Deprivation, reaching an R-squared of 0.583. The relationships proved spatially non-stationary: greenery perception is negatively associated with deprivation across most of the study area but the strength varies, and in places the sign reverses. A single citywide coefficient would therefore misstate the exposure for most neighborhoods, which is the finding that carries directly into the health projects.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig-full" style="margin:18px 0 20px">
        <div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/poverty_streetview-segmentation.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/poverty_streetview-segmentation.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Diagram: street view images at four headings pass through a PSPNet architecture producing segmented images and extracted open areas" width="2400" height="1148" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
        <figcaption><b>The measurement model.</b> Street view images captured at four headings around each location are segmented by PSPNet into sky, greenery, building, car, and road, from which perceived greenery and open space are extracted.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:0.879fr 0.879fr">
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/poverty_gwr-greenery.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/poverty_gwr-greenery.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Map of geographically weighted regression coefficients for percentage of greenery perception" width="1339" height="1524" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Local coefficients for greenery perception against deprivation.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig-full"><div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/poverty_gwr-openspace.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/sensing-place-function/poverty_gwr-openspace.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Map of geographically weighted regression coefficients for percentage of open-space perception" width="1339" height="1524" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
          <figcaption>Local coefficients for open-space perception, with a markedly different spatial pattern.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2020.101544">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/mengSensingUrbanPoverty2020.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/mengSensingUrbanPoverty2020.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>



  <section id="leads">
    <p class="eyebrow">Continuity</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Where this leads</h2>
    <div class="leads">
      <p>This project supplies the social half of the exposure layer. The parcel-level function, vibrancy, and deprivation measures built here are what allow a health analysis to distinguish neighborhoods that a land cover map treats as identical. The street view segmentation pipeline is the same one later applied to the food environment in the <a href="{{ '/projects/place-cognitive-health/' | relative_url }}">cognitive health studies</a>, and the finding that place effects are spatially non-stationary is what motivates the local rather than global models used in the <a href="{{ '/projects/place-covid-19-mortality/' | relative_url }}">COVID-19 mortality work</a>. Measuring urban function is not the endpoint: it is what makes a place-based exposure specific enough to be worth estimating.</p>
    </div>
    
  </section>

